# Project setup guide

This document explains how to set up, build, run, and publish the .NET8 Windows Forms application in this repository.

What this is
- Application type: Windows Forms (.NET8, target framework `net8.0-windows`)
- UI technology: WinForms (C#12)
- Data store: MySQL
- Data access: ADO.NET repositories (custom `AdoDbContext`) + EF Core-style migration classes that emit SQL (`MigrationBuilder.Sql`)
- File storage: local file system under `data/` (attachments, deliveries, project images)

Prerequisites
- Windows10/11
- Visual Studio2022 (17.8+) with:
 - Workload: Desktop development with .NET
- .NET8 SDK and runtime
- MySQL Server8.x (or compatible) and MySQL Workbench/CLI
- NuGet packages (restore will pull these automatically):
 - MySql.Data (Oracle) for ADO.NET
 - Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore (for migration base types)
 - Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational (for `MigrationBuilder`)
 - (Optional for EF tooling against MySQL) Pomelo.EntityFrameworkCore.MySql or Oracle EF provider

Clone and open
1) Clone the repository
2) Open `samp_01.sln` (or `samp_01.csproj`) in Visual Studio2022
3) Ensure the project targets `net8.0-windows`

Configuration
- Edit `AppConfig.cs` and set your MySQL connection string:
 - `public static string ConnectionString = "Server=localhost;Port=3306;Database=samp_01;Uid=...;Pwd=...;SslMode=None;";`
- Folders that the app will create/use at runtime:
 - `data/orders/{orderId}/attachments`
 - `data/orders/{orderId}/deliveries`

Database schema setup
Two supported approaches.

A) Run the SQL in migrations (manual)
- Migrations are in `Migrations/` and contain MySQL DDL/ALTER statements via `migrationBuilder.Sql(...)`.
- In order (idempotent):
1. `20251025_InitialCreate_AddUsersTable.cs` (creates `users`)
2. `20251025_AddServicesAndSellers.cs` (creates `services`, `sellers`, seeds sample services)
3. `20251029_AddOrdersMessagingPayments.cs` (creates `orders`, `messages`, `payments`)
4. `20251030_AlterMessages_ForDirectChat.cs` (alters `messages` for direct chat and indexes)
- Copy/paste the SQL strings from these files into MySQL Workbench and execute in your database.

B) Use EF Core tools to apply migrations (optional)
- Install tooling:
 - `dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef`
- Add a MySQL EF provider (choose one):
 - Pomelo: `dotnet add package Pomelo.EntityFrameworkCore.MySql`
 - Oracle: `dotnet add package MySql.EntityFrameworkCore` (provider name may vary)
- Then run from project directory:
 - `dotnet ef database update`
- Note: The migrations in this repo use raw SQL only; no model snapshots of entities are relied upon for table creation beyond the SQL. A provider is still needed for EF runtime.

Build and run
- Set `samp_01` as startup project
- Start (F5). The app uses the connection string in `AppConfig.cs` to access MySQL.
- First-run checks: verify the DB tables exist and credentials allow CRUD.

Accounts and seeding
- Admin: `Migrations/20251026_AddAdmins_SeedRoot.cs` seeds a default admin (name: `admin`, password: `Admin@123`).
- Services seed: `AddServicesAndSellers` inserts sample services (idempotent).
- Create users/providers via the provided registration forms or insert directly.

Project structure highlights
- `Forms/User/*`: User-facing dashboard, services browser, orders, direct messages
- `Forms/ServiceProvider/*`: Provider dashboard, portfolio, orders, direct messages
- `Services/*`: Business logic wrappers (orders, portfolio, login/registration)
- `Data/Repositories/*`: ADO.NET repositories for MySQL access
- `Migrations/*`: EF Migration classes containing MySQL SQL (idempotent)
- `Domain/*`: Entities and DTOs

Publish (distribute)
- Visual Studio: right-click project > Publish
 - Target: Folder
 - Runtime: `win-x64`
 - Deployment: Self-contained (includes .NET) or Framework-dependent (requires .NET8 Desktop Runtime)
 - Single-file: On
 - Trim: Off (WinForms uses reflection)
- CLI example (self-contained, single-file):
 - `dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 --self-contained true -p:PublishSingleFile=true -p:IncludeAllContentForSelfExtract=true -p:PublishTrimmed=false`
 - Output: `bin/Release/net8.0-windows/win-x64/publish`

Troubleshooting
- Cannot publish: ensure the app is closed (file lock on `samp_01.exe`).
- DB errors: verify connection string, server reachability, and that migration SQL was applied.
- Missing images/paths: ensure local files referenced in portfolios/projects exist on the machine.

Security notes
- Do not hard-code production credentials in source.
- Consider environment-specific config and secure secret storage.
- Validate/limit file types for attachments in production environments.
